you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's programme as global  temperatures  tick up the uk government is criticized  for not living up to its promises  on tackling climate change how switching  to more sustainable farming techniques  could help cut carbon emissions  and pointless by name but not by nature  we meet the founder  of an online plant shop with a  difference  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show  where we track the changes happening to  our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions now  9 out of 10 for ambition 4 out of 10  for policies to get there that is the  verdict of the latest report  from the independent climate change  committee which advises the government  it says that ministers have made  historic climate promises in the past  year for which  they deserve credit but have been far  too slow to follow these with detailed  plans and  delivery the committee made more than  200  recommendations including managing the  phase out of gas  boilers only building new roads if they  can be shown they won't increase  emissions  bringing forward regulations to ensure  all new homes are built to low carbon  standards and a public information  campaign  to explain to people the changes that  are needed such as cutting down  on the amount of meat we eat well the  report comes ahead of the crucial united  nations cop 26 climate summit  which the uk is hosting in november but  the chief executive of the climate  change committee chris stark  says more needs to be done before then  if government efforts are to be taken  seriously i think we have to give the  prime minister  and this government credit for setting  these ambitious targets now that has  been a big part of the last 12 months  we've set  a really ambitious set of targets for  2013 for 2035  to complement the net zero target that  we already have in the uk  but what we haven't seen from the  government is the kind of detailed plans  and how we will reach  those targets what the government seem  to be doing is looking out to  the weeks before cop cop26 to a new  strategy for net zero and they seem to  be  putting all of their emphasis on on the  production of that strategy and that's  quite  a high-stakes gamble well the  government's defended its policies and  ambition  in a statement a spokesperson said any  suggestion we have been  slow to deliver climate action is widely  off the mark  over the past three decades we have  driven down emissions by  44 the fastest reduction of  any g7 country our forthcoming  strategies on  heat and buildings hydrogen transport  and comprehensive net zero strategy this  year  will set out more of the very policies  the climate change committee is calling  for  as we redouble our efforts to end the  uk's contribution  to climate change well let's bring in  now chaitanya kumar head of  environment at the new economics  foundation  really good to talk to you there's a  pretty big  gap isn't there in that report between  the promises of the government and  delivery how large is that gap in your  estimation  well the government's climate advisors  the climate change committee  are fairly clear um they lay out a  pathway between  where we ought to be in about 14 years  and where we are now  and the gap is roughly based on existing  policies  we probably get about less than halfway  where we need to be by 2035.  so it's fairly significant the gap  between your rhetoric and reality  and there are certain bits of government  aren't there which are further off the  mark than others  that's right um the committee doesn't  hold back  in basically singling out the treasury  and uh and clg  in the ministry for housing and  communities for  really sort of being very slow on acting  on climate change and sometimes  actively blocking climate action i think  that's  quite a a charge especially with the  treasury because  the treasury as we all know holds the  burst strings for a lot of climate  action and funding  uh climate policy if treasury isn't  playing ball  then it's very difficult to see how we  will get ourselves get ourselves on  track to net zero  now there's a very long laundry list of  recommendations  um some 200 which of them do you think  could and should be implemented straight  away and indeed ahead of cop 26.  two things i'd be very interested in  personally one is on public engagement  that's been the biggest sort of missing  piece in this puzzle  we know changes have to be made but if  you don't level with people  that then we have a challenge we need to  let people adopt a low carbon lifestyle  and that won't happen  you know without government support heat  pumps electric vehicles won't subsidize  themselves  we need government intervention there  the second key recommendation i think  is how do we really support local and  regional authorities  i believe they're quite central to  achieving that serum but a decade of  austerity and lack of resources has  meant  what is an important lever in public  engagement and implementing solutions  is largely missing from the picture  chatanya kumar good to talk to you thank  you  thank you in today's other climate news  the uk firm storage and its canadian  partner carbon engineering have begun  designing a direct air facility  in scotland the companies say that the  plant will permanently remove between  five hundred thousand and one million  tons of carbon dioxide from the  atmosphere  every year it would be the first  large-scale facility of its kind in  europe  and aims to be fully operational by 2026  organisers of a new environmental  project in the yorkshire dales  say it will demonstrate how the uk's  uplands can be transformed  to help fight climate change the scheme  will re-establish peatlands  and create new woodland across 1200  hectares  of land around ingleborough to remove  and  store carbon the partnership behind the  project say  it's an important step ahead of the cop  26 summit in glasgow  due to take place in november  microsoft is being urged to use its own  video conferencing tools to keep its  corporate travel  at 2020 levels the just used teams  campaign  says that the company should set an  example to others by using its platform  teams instead of asking u.s employees to  fly to face-to-face meetings  campaigners claim microsoft employees  have their own  check-in lane at seattle airport  microsoft says it's committed to become  carbon negative by 2030.  and a remote uk overseas territory will  be given protected status to conserve  its wildlife  it will cover the entire area of south  georgia and the south  sandwich islands nearly 1500 square  miles  home to seabirds seals and vast colonies  of  penguins its government says it will  focus on preserving the island's  ecosystems  and protect biodiversity this week  thousands of farmers are coming together  to discuss innovative ways to make  farming more sustainable  and to help address climate change  globally it's estimated that  20 kilograms of topsoil is lost for  every  1 kilo of food produced from  industrialized farming  well that's because it relies on harmful  chemicals heavy machinery and ploughing  which damages the soil  and with more than 95 percent of our  food coming from the ground  it's vital that farmers protect and  nurture it  well they're being encouraged to move  towards agricultural methods which are  more  environmentally friendly these include  tilling farm fields less to help the  land  store co2 making use of renewable energy  sources  and planting a wider variety of crops  sustainable farming can use up to 56  less energy per unit of crops produced  and it creates  64 percent less co2 emissions per  hectare of land  one person who knows what a difference  sustainable farming can make is  thomasina myers  she's co-founder of the restaurant chain  oaxaca which was certified  carbon neutral in 2016 and  aims to use products from regenerative  farming thomasina good to talk to you  you must have to do a lot of work to  check out  exactly where your ingredients are  coming from  and what methods they use  yeah i mean that's a huge part of what  we do because we believe it's so  important  to put incredible ingredients on our  plates  not just because we believe it's vital  to support these farmers  who can play such a huge part in  mitigating climate change  and helping the government meet those  carbon targets but also because we  really believe that that food that they  produce  in better soil has way more nutrition in  it than  food producing these more conventional  methods  which are so bad for soil erosion the  soil that those  methods use is really depleted in  nutrition um as opposed to  you know healthy soil which really does  produce better food  we were saying in the introduction there  you went carbon neutral  back in 2016 that's before many other  restauranteurs i'd imagine  were on board what were the biggest  challenges for taking that path back  then  so i think actually it's been a  wonderful journey we've worked with  we work across our board from uh from  how we build our restaurants how we  recycle our materials  we recycle our food waste we take the  hot or  air from our fridges and freezes and  divert it heat up our hot water  so there's actually really lovely kind  of fun ways you can mitigate climate  change  in that way and then you know in terms  of offsetting carbon  there are really great projects you can  get on board and we try and make ours  back to mexico where we can i think the  great piece here is that if the  government is clever farmers can be such  a huge part  in climate mitigation because healthy  soil stores  huge amounts of carbon we're talking  about the rainforest  you know equalizing so we can't grow  rainforests here but we can have really  brilliantly healthy soil  and with the government's new food  strategy if they can really get behind  us  the farmers to help them do the the best  thing  these farmers want to do the best thing  they want to be taking their part in  creating biodiversity having holistic  farming methods where they're  diverting monoculture into you know  multicultural so they're  they're just promoting diversity helping  soil health and producing  much better food that's so interesting  talking to you thomasina myers thank you  for your time thank you  now what are your goals for the next  four years well for one former pilot  he's got  lofty ambitions to plant a million trees  by 2025. nathan rob founded pointless  plants to try and offer a more  sustainable option  for those looking to test out their  green fingers by banning plastic pots  peat-based soil and plant wastage here's  his story  welcome to my pointless plants crib  hi guys i'm nathan rob i'm the founder  of pointless plants  here is all of our plants we're on a  mission to plant  one million trees by the end of 2025 for  every order we plant  10 trees i'm 24 years old and i was a  pilot for four years  flying around spraying tons and tons of  fuel  i've seen big organizations and  companies putting profits over peak in  the planets  that really just did not fulfill me so  this is a plant i love  it's a clayton lancifolia there's a lot  of  information out there for example we  plant entries for every order  and we do this to reduce carbon and  there's always so much new information  now it looks like  algae might be a better way to produce  carbon there's so much information it's  sometimes  hard to know what the right thing to do  but i really feel like small  changes can make a really big difference  so planting 10 trees better than plant  no trees  i believe it's my responsibility to do  what i can do to try and make this  planet a greener place  that's it for today's show coming up on  the daily climate show tomorrow  our correspondent adam parsons reports  on the villages in germany  being demolished to make way for the  expansion of a vast coal mine  you can see that at the same time  tomorrow here on sky news  or watch the full show on youtube hope  to see you then  you

